/*  Thinking process:
    1)  “Perfect match” at customer level requires equal (a) number of orders vs invoices
        and (b) total value of those orders vs invoices.  Previous strict filters returned
        zero rows, so first we need to *see* where the mismatches occur.
    2)  Build two separate aggregates:
          • order_summary:   one row per CustomerID with COUNT(DISTINCT OrderID) and the
                             monetary total derived from SUM(Quantity × UnitPrice).
          • invoice_summary: one row per CustomerID with COUNT(DISTINCT InvoiceID) and the
                             monetary total derived from SUM(ExtendedPrice) while excluding
                             credit notes (IsCreditNote = 0).
    3)  Join the two summaries on CustomerID.  Instead of filtering for equality, expose
        the differences so we can inspect how close or far each customer is.
    4)  LIMIT 20 keeps the output manageable for manual inspection.
*/
WITH order_summary AS (
    SELECT
        so."CustomerID",
        COUNT(DISTINCT so."OrderID")            AS "OrderCnt",
        SUM(ol."Quantity" * ol."UnitPrice")     AS "OrderValue"
    FROM
        "WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS"."WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS"."SALES_ORDERS"      so
    JOIN
        "WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS"."WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS"."SALES_ORDERLINES"  ol
